  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="two-runs" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.1 · do not conflate</span>
        <h2>Today’s laboratory is not tomorrow’s control plane.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="run-mode-cards">
        <article class="run-mode-current">
          <div>{{ status("current", "Current") }}<span>one executable</span></div>
          <h3><code>dekopon-run prompt</code></h3>
          <p>A synchronous one-shot developer path. It performs blocking model HTTP and import-free read-only component calls in one process. It does not authorize anything.</p>
          <ul><li>one <code>dekopon-run</code> OS process</li><li>main thread performs orchestration and guest execution</li><li>short-lived deadline thread per component call</li><li>model endpoint is out of process</li></ul>
        </article>
        <article class="run-mode-future">
          <div>{{ status("direction", "Committed direction") }}<span>separate deployment units</span></div>
          <h3><code>dekopond</code> → <code>dekopon-brokerd</code></h3>
          <p>An unprivileged orchestration daemon sends an authenticated proposal to a separately deployed broker. The broker alone evaluates policy, holds provider credentials, executes a constrained provider, and records evidence.</p>
          <ul><li>at least two OS processes and pods for writes</li><li>authorization created and consumed inside broker boundary</li><li>future async Wasmtime host integrated with Tokio</li><li>exact transport and worker topology remain open</li></ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>“One Tokio process” has a security limit.</strong>
        <p>Multiple agent tasks and Wasm stores may share one Tokio runtime <em>inside a role</em>. Once external-write authority exists, Dekopon’s design forbids collapsing orchestration and broker authority into one process. The daemon and broker remain separate even if each is internally concurrent.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

