    <section id="current-threads" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.3 · current execution inventory</span>
        <h2>What actually runs where today.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table thread-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Work</th><th scope="col">OS process</th><th scope="col">Thread / context</th><th scope="col">Blocking?</th><th scope="col">Lifetime</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">CLI, registry, prompt loop</th><td><code>dekopon-run</code></td><td>main OS thread</td><td>Yes, synchronous stack</td><td>whole command</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Component compilation</th><td><code>dekopon-run</code></td><td>called from main thread; Wasmtime implementation may use internal compiler facilities</td><td>Caller waits</td><td>once per component load</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Model HTTP / SSE</th><td><code>dekopon-run</code></td><td>main thread in blocking <code>ureq</code> client</td><td>Yes</td><td>one model turn</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Guest <code>describe</code> / <code>invoke</code></th><td><code>dekopon-run</code></td><td>main thread enters a Wasmtime Store</td><td>Yes; runtime mutex held</td><td>fresh Store + instance per call</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Wall-clock watchdog</th><td><code>dekopon-run</code></td><td>temporary named OS thread</td><td>Waits on channel</td><td>one per component call</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">LLM inference</th><td>model server process</td><td>owned by Ollama/OpenAI/model service</td><td>Host waits for it</td><td>outside Dekopon</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>There is no Tokio runtime in <code>dekopon-run</code> on this snapshot. The Wasmtime Cargo dependency also omits its <code>async</code> feature. Fuel currently sets a deterministic instruction budget; epoch interruption plus a helper thread turns the wall-clock deadline into a trap. Neither mechanism currently yields a guest so another agent task can run.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="future-boundary" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.4 · planned deployment</span>
        <h2>Concurrency inside; isolation between.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="future-topology">
        <article>
          <div><span>PROCESS A</span>{{ status("direction", "Committed role") }}</div>
          <h3><code>dekopond</code></h3>
          <p>Owns model interaction, context, memory, and unprivileged task coordination. It may run many agent tasks, but it holds no provider credentials and cannot manufacture authorization.</p>
          <strong>Data owned here</strong><ul><li>conversation and task state</li><li>model credentials inside the selected model client</li><li>untrusted proposed invocation</li></ul>
        </article>
        <div class="topology-crossing"><span>authenticated envelope</span><strong>proposal →</strong><small>not an authorized bearer grant</small></div>
        <article>
          <div><span>PROCESS B</span>{{ status("direction", "Committed role") }}</div>
          <h3><code>dekopon-brokerd</code></h3>
          <p>Authenticates the envelope, resolves trusted identity, evaluates policy, creates and consumes constrained authorization, invokes the provider, and records evidence.</p>
          <strong>Data owned here</strong><ul><li>trusted actor and policy decision</li><li>provider credentials and constraints</li><li>authorization receipt, result, evidence, audit</li></ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p>Within the broker process, a shared Wasmtime engine and compiled component cache can amortize compilation. Every invocation still gets a fresh bounded Store. Tokio tasks can overlap network waits and independent invocations, subject to explicit admission limits; sharing an Engine does not imply sharing Store state.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="authority-transition" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.5 · security state machine</span>
        <h2>The crucial object never crosses back.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="authority-state-machine">
        <div><span>daemon may construct</span><strong><code>ProposedInvocation</code></strong><small>untrusted intent + arguments</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">authenticated request →</i>
        <div class="authority-broker-box">
          <span>inside broker execution boundary</span>
          <div><strong>authenticate</strong><b>→</b><strong>evaluate policy</strong><b>→</b><strong>create <code>AuthorizedInvocation</code></strong><b>→</b><strong>execute</strong></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→ result + evidence</i>
        <div><span>daemon may receive</span><strong><code>InvocationResult</code></strong><small>authorization is not returned</small></div>
      </div>

      <p>The future daemon-to-broker wire carries a proposal and trusted envelope context. It does not carry a serialized <code>AuthorizedInvocation</code> for the broker to trust, and the broker does not return transferable authorization to the daemon. Private Rust fields help prevent accidental in-process fabrication; process isolation and authenticated execution provide the real control.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="open-design" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03.6 · unresolved on purpose</span>
        <h2>What the diagram does not decide.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="open-question-grid">
        <article><span>transport</span><h3>How do processes talk?</h3><p>Unix socket, local RPC, mTLS, identity format, freshness, replay defense, and protocol negotiation are not implemented.</p></article>
        <article><span>executor</span><h3>Which threads poll Wasm?</h3><p>General Tokio workers, a dedicated runtime, or a bounded executor pool remain options. CPU and memory budgets must drive the choice.</p></article>
        <article><span>fairness</span><h3>Fuel or epoch yields?</h3><p>Deterministic fuel and lower-overhead epoch time-slicing have different cost and fairness properties. The next chapter simulates both concepts.</p></article>
        <article><span>persistence</span><h3>Where does audit live?</h3><p>Append ordering, evidence canonicalization, key management, rollback defense, retention, and incident workflows still need design.</p></article>
      </div>

